WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.740
The establishment left has just dropped entirely.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.509
So the democratic party, whatever that is,

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:14.110
abandoned the working class a generation ago.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.580
Okay.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.580
Let's get straight into the questions.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:19.580
Is that all right?

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.580
Oh yeah.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.580
Okay.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.939
So Peter, you can mute your mic if you don't
mind.

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:24.939
Got it.

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:25.939
Okay.

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:26.939
What I wanted to know is the first,

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.199
I wanted to define the left and essentially
the way that I'm going to do that

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:34.820
is by asking you, what are the traits in the
left, the left,

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.890
political left in the past century that have
changed and what's remained the

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.910
same as far as you can tell?

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.469
Well, of course,

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:47.390
the left has moved consistently to new

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.160
issues that weren't even noticed before.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.239
So if you go back a century, well,

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:58.479
there were elements of the left that you could
call, say, feminist.

00:00:58.479 --> 00:01:01.060
It's nothing like the dominant,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.619
the dominant element in left

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:07.950
politics that exists today.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.170
You go back 20 years ago,

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.930
there was very little concern about the environment.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:19.940
Now the left recognizes that it's an existential
problem.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.770
On the other hand,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.890
and so there are many cases in which there's
been progress in understanding

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.530
and dedicated work.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.030
There are other cases in which there's regression.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.130
So go back not very far,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.010
a couple of decades,

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.729
labor organizing was a major issue for the
left.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:48.899
It was recognized and understood that the
driving force in any

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:53.780
social and political economic change is going
to be an organized working class.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.220
That's been,

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.340
well, it depends what you call the left,

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.530
but the establishment left has just dropped
it entirely.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.560
So the democratic party,

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:07.940
whatever that is,

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.290
abandoned the working class a generation ago,

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.140
handed it over to their class enemy.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.019
Activists on the left have become engaged

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:24.590
justifiably with issues that are sometimes
called identity

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:25.590
politics.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.700
Those are real issues, have to be dealt with.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.099
But class issues have been subordinated,

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:36.019
marginalized, and they're very real.

00:02:36.019 --> 00:02:40.090
That's a deficiency that has to be overcome.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:44.990
All of these things interact when people talk
about intersectionality,

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.310
yes, they're right,

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:52.360
but it's been more of a slogan than an actual
achievement.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:58.510
So I think if you look across the board, there's
progress and regression.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.390
What is your opinion on identity politics?

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:05.620
The issues are significant.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.500
They have to, they cannot,

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:15.480
each type of identity politics cannot dominate

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.000
a commitment of an organized left that hopes
to achieve things.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.590
They have to interact,

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:26.870
be mutually supportive and they have to crucially
bring in the class issues that

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.200
have been subordinated.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:37.670
Actually the most active extreme form of identity
politics is white nationalism.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.630
Something I'm trying to determine with my
research is when does the left go too

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:45.129
far and when does the right go too far, politically
speaking?

00:03:45.129 --> 00:03:47.540
And I wanted to know what your opinion is
on that.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.439
Just to pick the issues.

00:03:49.439 --> 00:03:54.349
I mean, the right at this point is simply
suicidal.

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:58.980
It's not a question of going too far.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:04.540
The right is committed to destruction of organized
human society.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.220
Is that an extreme statement?

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.230
Maybe, but it's correct.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.379
Just take a look at the Republican party.

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:13.670
What is the Republican party committed to?

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:19.930
It's committed to destroying human life and
not in the long term.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:24.200
The Paris agreements a couple of years ago,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.530
they weren't fabulous, but they were something
at least.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.789
Their original effort intent was to destroy
the human race.

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:39.009
It was to create a treaty in which there would
be verifiable commitments to some

00:04:39.009 --> 00:04:43.590
measures to avert the huge threat of global
warming.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.449
Couldn't get a treaty.

00:04:45.449 --> 00:04:49.800
It had to be a voluntary agreement.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:50.949
Why?

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:53.729
Republican senators wouldn't accept it.

00:04:53.729 --> 00:04:58.949
I take the Republican primary in 2016.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:05.750
Every single candidate, without exception,
either denied that global warming is taking

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:06.750
place

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.780
or else said, maybe it is, but we don't care.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:16.400
John Kasich, who was the governor of Ohio,
who's considered the real moderate of the

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.400
group,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.340
said, yeah, we recognize that global warming
is taking place and it's serious,

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:28.460
but we in Ohio are going to use our coal and
we're not going to apologize for it.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.590
That's the moderate Republican.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.090
Then you go over to the White House.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:34.150
You have total maniacs.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.720
People who say we don't care.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.850
I said, we'll destroy the world, but we'll
have profit tomorrow and are leading the way

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.410
towards destruction, consciously aware of
what they're doing.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:46.650
No secret about it.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:47.650
Easy to demonstrate.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.000
How far to the right is this?

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.020
I mean, there's no words for it.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.300
One question.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.419
What about the left?

00:05:56.419 --> 00:05:58.100
When does the left go too far?

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.979
It's not a matter of going too far.

00:06:00.979 --> 00:06:07.180
It's a matter of making tactical decisions
that are incorrect.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:13.380
You have to, if you, you may, there's, it
may be that some of the goals go to, are debatable.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.820
Talk about that.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:20.090
But insofar as they're the kinds of goals
that, say, we think we can support,

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:26.539
you have to ask whether the tactics that are
used are well designed to meet those goals

00:06:26.539 --> 00:06:28.690
or else undermine the goals.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.280
So let's take concrete cases.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:39.740
As you know, I was very active in the anti-war
movement, resistance movement during the 1960s.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:41.780
I had contact with Vietnamese.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:46.880
The Vietnamese were appalled by some of the
tactics that were being used.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:55.290
I remember meetings where the Vietnamese would
plead with American activists not to do things

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.330
like what the weathermen are doing.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:03.620
The weathermen, a lot of young people, many
of them I knew, they were perfectly sincere.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.440
They thought that the way to end the war is
to smash up the windows on Main Street.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.590
It's not the way to end the war.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:16.890
That was the way to enrage construction workers
and others, so they'd be more pro-war than

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.780
they were before.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:23.440
The Vietnamese didn't care whether Americans
felt good.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:25.190
They cared whether they could survive.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:33.110
So what they advocated were measures so mild
that a lot of the war movement would just

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.110
laugh at them.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.069
But that's the kind of choice that has to
be made all the time.

00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:40.009
I can give you many examples today.

00:07:40.009 --> 00:07:46.669
So going too far, I think the way we should
rephrase that is picking tactics that are

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:48.060
going to undermine

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:55.520
the perhaps legitimate and just goals that
you say you're advocating.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:03.190
On the discussion of the left and the right,
in terms of their differences and their similarities,

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:08.169
oftentimes the left are characterized as being
pro-government and the right is characterized

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:09.260
as being pro-corporation.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:15.351
And you have written about the revolving door
between the two, between regulators and the

00:08:15.351 --> 00:08:19.690
regulated, in your book like Manufacturing
Consent.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:25.260
Now, does this blur the line of what it means
to be a left-winger and a right-winger?

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.909
Do the left and the right still exist?

00:08:28.909 --> 00:08:31.470
Did they ever exist?

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.269
I mean, let's take being pro- or anti-government.

00:08:34.269 --> 00:08:39.669
That's not a general position of the left.

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:44.279
That's a tactical choice in particular circumstances.

00:08:44.279 --> 00:08:53.690
So when you have a state like the United States
that's largely dominated by private tyrannies

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:56.640
with very little public voice,

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:05.750
in that particular circumstance, the option
available to you to overcome this is governmental

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:06.750
action,

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:10.780
which at least to some extent is responsive
to public opinion.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:20.260
In other circumstances, when you're trying
to construct a really libertarian left participatory

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:21.380
society,

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:26.280
you might want to dissolve governmental structures
as a legitimate authority.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.850
There's no right or wrong answer to that.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:34.410
So, for example, let's take, say, media.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.089
The United States is unusual.

00:09:37.089 --> 00:09:42.070
It's different from other developed societies
in many respects.

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:46.829
One respect is it doesn't have public, barely
has public media.

00:09:46.829 --> 00:09:49.200
Media are privatized, overwhelming.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.399
It's kind of a little tiny fringe on the left,
nothing like, off the fringe.

00:09:53.399 --> 00:09:58.000
So it's like nothing like the BBC or anything
like that.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.120
We can look at why this happened.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:08.700
Certainly not implicit in U.S. history, like
the founders of the country, the framers,

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:13.290
believe they interpreted the First Amendment
very differently than the way we do.

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:18.579
They thought it meant that the government
ought to take an active role in sponsoring

00:10:18.579 --> 00:10:20.250
a free and independent press.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.000
That's why we have the post office, for example.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:31.050
In its early years, the post office was almost
totally devoted to providing a cheap,

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:37.399
free, basically subsidies to a wide variety
of journals and newspapers

00:10:37.399 --> 00:10:42.510
to encourage a varied, free, independent press.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:48.240
Over the years, the power of private power
in the United States,

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:56.510
which is quite unusual, managed to privatize
radio, television and more recently, the Internet.

00:10:56.510 --> 00:11:01.480
But that's and yes, in those circumstances,
it would make sense to call for a public voice.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:07.340
Under other circumstances, you might want
to have local, local

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:13.339
based, you know, worker based, community based
media.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:15.529
And there's many other such questions.

00:11:15.529 --> 00:11:20.000
You're a self-described anarchist, and I'd
like to know what is the difference between

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:23.769
your beliefs and contemporary libertarianism?

00:11:23.769 --> 00:11:33.700
Well, first of all, what's called libertarian
in the United States is some ultra right advocacy

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:35.709
of private tyrannies.

00:11:35.709 --> 00:11:40.990
Nothing, nothing like anything that was libertarian
traditionally.

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:44.019
But if you're talking about left libertarian,
it's.

00:11:44.019 --> 00:11:50.630
You know, covers quite a range, but just as
the term anarchism does.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:54.720
But there is a kind of a core at the center
of it, I think.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:12:01.100
The core running through the whole tradition,
many variations,

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:06.450
is the recognition that there are certain
structures

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:15.330
in social, political life, economic life that
are that have to justify their own legitimacy.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.170
They are coercive, authoritarian.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:24.649
There is hierarchy, domination.

00:12:24.649 --> 00:12:27.940
Somebody gives you or somebody takes them.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:32.530
All of those structures have to be are none
of those structures are self-justifying.

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.230
They have a burden of justification.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:40.639
If they can't meet it, which is almost always
the case, they should be dismantled.

00:12:40.639 --> 00:12:42.650
I think that's the core principle.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:49.240
And my own view is that once you get to the
basics, almost every normal person is an anarchist

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:50.600
in this sense.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:53.410
But that's then you have to ask how it applies.

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.450
So do you have a job somewhere?

00:12:55.450 --> 00:13:03.820
Well, if you have a job in a business corporation,
let's say you're living in a tyranny.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:08.220
So extreme that no totalitarian dictator ever
dreamed of it.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:14.720
So, for example, Stalin didn't tell people
that you have 10 minutes to go to the bathroom

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:15.720
every couple hours.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.339
You have to wear these clothes, not some other
clothes.

00:13:18.339 --> 00:13:22.200
You're not allowed to stop to talk to a friend
for a minute.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.380
You're monitored, say, at an Amazon warehouse.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:32.529
So that if you don't pick up enough things
fast enough, like maybe you stop to breathe

00:13:32.529 --> 00:13:33.529
or something,

00:13:33.529 --> 00:13:34.529
you get a demerit.

00:13:34.529 --> 00:13:36.660
There was no totalitarian dictatorship like
that.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:37.839
That's most of people's lives.

00:13:37.839 --> 00:13:40.470
Well, is that a legitimate structure?

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:44.280
Is the labor contract legitimate?

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:49.000
Actually, traditional classical liberals didn't
think so.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.880
Abraham Lincoln didn't think so.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:53.950
The Republican Party didn't think so.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:58.459
The Republican Party's slogan in the mid-19th
century, early Industrial Revolution, was

00:13:58.459 --> 00:13:59.759
that

00:13:59.759 --> 00:14:06.940
wage labor is indistinguishable from slavery,
except that it's supposed to be temporary

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.990
until you become a free person again.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:14.511
Well, OK, those ideas have been driven out
of people's heads, but I don't think they're

00:14:14.511 --> 00:14:16.639
very far below the surface.

00:14:16.639 --> 00:14:18.720
They can emerge.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.980
And that would be authentic left libertarianism,
just one aspect.

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:25.860
Crucial aspect.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:30.990
So it seems like you overlap with regards
to free exchange of speech and ideas.

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:36.089
And I remember a while ago you helped, you
defended a Holocaust denier.

00:14:36.089 --> 00:14:40.260
And I wanted to know if you still stand by
that.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.329
Defending a Holocaust denier?

00:14:41.329 --> 00:14:43.230
I mean, defending his freedom of speech.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:44.230
Sure.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:48.269
I mean, this is just standard classical liberalism.

00:14:48.269 --> 00:14:50.690
There's two choices.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:59.839
Either some form of power and authority, typically
the state or other, determines what's true

00:14:59.839 --> 00:15:05.480
and punishes any deviation from what it claims
is true.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:09.600
Or else you allow views to be expressed that
you don't like.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:15.180
In fact, if you really believe in freedom
of speech, the only issues are, do I allow

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:18.470
speech that I don't like?

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:24.529
Hitler and Stalin had nothing against speech
that they liked.

00:15:24.529 --> 00:15:30.190
Nowadays, there's this trans rights versus
free speech debate.

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:32.150
And I want to know what you think about that.

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:33.459
Where do you lie on that?

00:15:33.459 --> 00:15:38.139
Let's talk about Holocaust denial for another
minute.

00:15:38.139 --> 00:15:43.620
The Holocaust denial is the norm in Western
society.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:53.470
So, just to take an example, a couple of years
ago, an article appeared in the New York Review,

00:15:53.470 --> 00:16:01.089
the major journal of left liberalism, in which
the author, good, decent left liberal, was

00:16:01.089 --> 00:16:04.620
reviewing a book by a major American historian.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:13.870
He said in his review, he was interested to
learn that when the early explorers came to

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:21.600
the Western Hemisphere, there were only about
a million people from the tropical forests

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:22.810
to the frozen north.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:28.740
He was off by about 60 or 70 million who were
wiped out.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.589
Is that Holocaust denial?

00:16:31.589 --> 00:16:33.459
Did anybody say he should be imprisoned?

00:16:33.459 --> 00:16:36.160
Did anybody even notice?

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.400
I mean, these things happen all the time.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:46.930
It's a particular form that we don't like,
that somebody doesn't like, that's considered,

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:48.990
that has to be suppressed.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:53.089
And that's worth keeping in mind when you
talk about Holocaust denial.

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:59.140
So, it seems like you have some similarities
with Jordan Peterson on this issue.

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:02.449
And I know that you haven't talked much about
Jordan Peterson.

00:17:02.449 --> 00:17:06.730
And I wanted to know if you had any disagreements
with what he says.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.490
Frankly, I pay very little attention.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:16.370
But if you want to know about Jordan Peterson,
I think the best thing I can refer you to

00:17:16.370 --> 00:17:24.110
is an article by Nathan Robinson, a very sharp,
acute critic in his journal Current Affairs.

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:29.930
It's called something like The Intellectual
We Deserve or something like that.

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:32.390
I think that's basically my answer.

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:37.440
Other than that, I don't pay any attention.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:41.200
I mean, there's some issues on which I probably
agree with Hitler.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:44.789
No, it doesn't mean much.

00:17:44.789 --> 00:17:51.070
On that subject, I think it's Aristotle, right,
who says that people get the governments they

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:52.890
deserve.

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:54.700
Is this kind of the line?

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:58.140
Aristotle said that people get the governments
they deserve.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:02.020
Is this kind of what you're implying, but
for intellectuals?

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:05.950
No, I don't think people get the government
they deserve.

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:09.440
They get the government that power systems
impose.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:11.960
That's quite different.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:17.840
On the systems of power, you had mentioned
in Manufacturing Consent the idea of the principle

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:24.770
of bureaucratic affinity, the idea that large
scale bureaucracies will ally with other large

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:31.140
scale bureaucracies to sort of maintain a
status quo and to aid each other.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:37.090
And that when a company like when a media
company, for example, tries true journalism,

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:42.559
these large bureaucracies be either the government
or corporations will give the media company

00:18:42.559 --> 00:18:48.450
flack and sort of cease to work with them.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:56.070
My question for you is, does the principle
of bureaucratic affinity apply to educational

00:18:56.070 --> 00:19:02.340
institutions like large universities?

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:05.590
To a varying extent.

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:09.190
Depends on the university, depends on the
time and the era.

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:18.950
You know, there's in our current situation,
current circumstances with all their flaws,

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:28.140
the universities are one of the last bastions
of relative freedom of expression and research.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:32.530
Even of worker self-government to a certain
extent.

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:40.820
Faculty at a university has a degree of control
over their working lives, which is very rare

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:44.800
in the existing socio-political system.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:48.230
So I don't think you can answer a simple yes
or no.

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:51.630
By comparative standards, they're relatively
free.

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:53.230
They have flaws.

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:59.120
So, for example, if you take a look at the
American university system, well, about just

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:06.460
a few years ago, you couldn't find a Marxist
professor anywhere.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:11.750
Any other country in the world, they'd be
all over the place.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.510
Not here.

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:18.290
May I ask what do you attribute that to?

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:24.930
In your in past comments, you had mentioned
how academics maintain a sort of status quo

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:30.740
as opposed to being the sort of radicals that
they display themselves as.

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.460
Do you find that still true?

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:37.530
Or do you find that academics are changing?

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:43.690
Well, and there's a kind of an ebb and flow.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:48.690
I think it's less true than it was 50 or 60
years ago.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:51.490
But it's more true than it ought to be.

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:53.429
And it's not just academics.

00:20:53.429 --> 00:20:55.540
It's intellectuals generally.

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:59.960
Look over the whole history of intellectuals
for a couple of thousand years, in fact.

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:00.960
And you find that overwhelmingly, they tend
to stay in the middle.

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:02.230
A couple of thousand years, in fact.

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:05.330
And you find that overwhelmingly, they tend
to support power systems.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.929
There are a few who don't.

00:21:08.929 --> 00:21:11.000
They're usually treated pretty harshly.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:18.419
In fact, just take the term intellectual in
its modern sense, was first used at the time

00:21:18.419 --> 00:21:22.020
of the Dreyfus trial in France, late 19th
century.

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:29.419
Most of the intellectuals, the prestigious
intellectuals, the ones of the French Academy

00:21:29.419 --> 00:21:32.930
and so on, bitterly condemned the Dreyfus
arts.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:40.030
How dare these writers and artists question
the majesty of our army, state and so on.

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:46.039
And there were a few like Emil Zola, a couple
of others who stood up against it.

00:21:46.039 --> 00:21:47.110
They were persecuted.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.850
And we may honor them today, but not at the
time.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:53.600
And Emil Zola had to flee France.

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:57.150
That's the record in one form or another,
all through history.

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:02.650
So, in fact, shortly after this, the First
World War came along.

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:10.150
It was very dramatic to see what happened
to the intellectual classes during this, including

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.850
the left, during the First World War.

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:20.100
In every single country, Germany, France,
England, the United States, the intellectuals

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:27.140
lined up almost 100 percent in passionate
support of their own country.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:30.710
The ones who didn't, many of them ended up
in jail.

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:35.750
Bertrand Russell, Rosa Luxemburg, Gene Debs.

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:36.900
That's the pattern.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:40.560
We're back, we're back.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.630
Peter, do you want to repeat the question?

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:44.760
Super quick.

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:46.720
This is my wife.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:49.179
Hi, thank you.

00:22:49.179 --> 00:22:50.179
Thank you.

00:22:50.179 --> 00:22:51.870
Hello, Professor Noam Chomsky's wife.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:52.870
Okay.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:23:01.960
The question in summary was, in Manufacturing
Consent, you teach us that big media is allied

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:03.809
with the government, corporate elites.

00:23:03.809 --> 00:23:10.700
As a result, the framework of big media's
discussion fits comfortably in a propaganda

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:16.340
model, an acceptable framework on how to analyze
problems and talk about issues.

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:24.919
Well, if academia itself is allied with government
and corporate elites, then wouldn't they also

00:23:24.919 --> 00:23:26.929
fit a propaganda model?

00:23:26.929 --> 00:23:30.340
And if not, what makes them so unique?

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:36.070
Well, for one thing, there's several things
that are, to some extent, it's true.

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:43.240
But notice that the relation of media to,
say, corporate elites is very different from

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:46.340
the relation of universities to corporate
elites.

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:49.970
In the case of media, it's just, that's what
they are.

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:55.110
The media are major corporations, parts of
bigger corporations.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:24:04.630
Flow in and out of government, you know, very
heavily subject to government edicts and so

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:05.630
on.

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:08.220
Universities have the same influences, but
much less so.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:13.680
The relation of a university to corporations
is, can I get a grant?

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:19.220
You know, will I get a, will there be a donor
who will be willing to build a building?

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:25.280
That's very different from being part of the
corporate system, which already allows a little

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:26.280
flexibility.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:34.710
Also, there is, we should not underestimate
the fact that there is a sense of professionalism

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:39.120
and intellectual responsibility.

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.340
That's true.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:46.700
There are serious journalists, many of them
who understand the system very well.

00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:51.270
They didn't have to hear it from us and who
try to find ways to combat it.

00:24:51.270 --> 00:24:57.960
And it happens now in the media, they tend
to be marginalized and eliminated.

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:00.160
You know, they don't have to be, you know,
they don't have to be in the media.

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:05.399
You know, sent to the police desk, you're
not really ready for big time work, you know,

00:25:05.399 --> 00:25:06.399
that sort of thing.

00:25:06.399 --> 00:25:12.309
And that kind of thing happens in the universities
too, but probably to a slightly lesser extent.

00:25:12.309 --> 00:25:14.970
And it depends also on the field.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:20.960
So many fields in the university are almost
totally free from outside pressures.

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:24.210
The physics department, for instance.

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:29.740
Some people have suggested that they should,
that we should remove universities.

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:33.330
And this is reminiscent, Jordan Peterson,
for example, said, suggests to students, don't

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:38.230
go to universities anymore because of the
bias, because of the unitary point of views.

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:42.490
And this is reminiscent of anarchist Yvonne
Illman, who said, you know, we should not

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:43.490
go to universities.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:46.169
Listen to what Jordan Peterson is saying.

00:25:46.169 --> 00:25:49.030
He's saying universities are dominated by
the left.

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:53.850
Now for him, the left, is anybody to the left
of Attila the Hun.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:57.250
In fact, universities are dominated by the
right.

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:00.912
He's so far on the right that that looks like
the left to him.

00:26:00.912 --> 00:26:02.039
And so he's saying, you know, we should eliminate
universities.

00:26:02.039 --> 00:26:03.039
But the right is people who will say that
university is dominated by the left.

00:26:03.039 --> 00:26:04.980
But does it make sense to tell students not
to go to universities?

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:05.980
It's crazy.

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:10.760
It's one of the places where you can, there's
lots of things wrong,

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:16.750
but there are resources and opportunities
that simply don't exist anywhere else.

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:24.060
You can't, there are opportunities to become
a free, independent, creative individual,

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.960
working with others that you just don't have
elsewhere.

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:28.860
Sorry, I misspoke.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:33.640
I didn't mean that to suggest that he suggested
to eliminate the universities.

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:37.240
I'm more trying to convey the decentralizing
of universities.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:38.990
So for example, an online school.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:44.200
There's a lot you miss in an online school.

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:46.039
Your peers.

00:26:46.039 --> 00:26:51.220
One of the big parts of education is the students
you're with.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:55.100
That those kinds of interactions are gone
online.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:27:00.250
And they're very important for educating oneself.

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:05.720
Anybody who's been in a, through a school
or university situation knows how you can

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:11.059
learn more from interaction with your peers
than from sitting in on a lecture.

00:27:11.059 --> 00:27:13.210
Even interaction with the faculty.

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:18.000
So it's one thing to sit in a lecture class
or watch a television screen.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:23.630
And it's another thing to be in an actual
class where you're interacting with other

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:25.429
students and with the faculty.

00:27:25.429 --> 00:27:27.330
So yes, there are good things.

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:33.580
I mean, I think it's good to try to extend
the resources of the university elsewhere.

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:39.750
But there's nothing to replace the direct
face to face interaction.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:43.470
Not just when you're in class, but when you're
doing something else.

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:49.720
When you're sitting in a McDonald's and having
a hamburger with your friends and talking.

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:55.440
What are your thoughts on the Christian anarchist,
Ivan Illich, and his rise in popularity amongst

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:57.320
social activists?

00:27:57.320 --> 00:28:00.909
An anarchist who believed in de-schooling
society.

00:28:00.909 --> 00:28:03.559
His popular work.

00:28:03.559 --> 00:28:07.920
I sort of understand some of the motivation
for it.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:16.380
But de-schooling society is taking away from
people some of their major opportunities for

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:19.640
individual growth and social interaction.

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:22.419
And even general activism.

00:28:22.419 --> 00:28:31.649
I mean, it's not just pure accident that over
the years, student activism has been typically

00:28:31.649 --> 00:28:37.830
at the forefront of many of the most important
social movements.

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:42.649
Partly it's just because young people are
a period of their life when they're relatively

00:28:42.649 --> 00:28:43.649
free.

00:28:43.649 --> 00:28:46.420
But it's also the fact that they're together.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:48.280
They can talk with one another.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.120
They can interact.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:56.720
Something that's pretty much missing and atomized
in capitalist society.

00:28:56.720 --> 00:29:00.190
Then I'm running into a little bit of a confusion
here.

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:06.769
On the one hand, the history of intellectuals
and academia sort of lines up with the status

00:29:06.769 --> 00:29:07.769
quo.

00:29:07.769 --> 00:29:12.320
In the sense that we were talking about Nazism
and things before.

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:16.720
But on the other hand, they're on the forefront
of social change.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:21.049
How are these views compatible?

00:29:21.049 --> 00:29:25.870
Their participants are in the forefront of
social change.

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:31.320
The mainstream may be status quo and conservative,
which it is.

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:32.650
Take a look at student activism.

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:37.120
It's usually opposed by the administration
and most of the faculty.

00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:39.620
But nevertheless, it takes place.

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:43.169
Because this is a relatively free institution.

00:29:43.169 --> 00:29:52.799
Which means that there are opportunities to
break out of the doctrinaire system of attempted

00:29:52.799 --> 00:29:54.720
regimentation.

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:59.910
We're not living in totalitarian dictatorships
where if you say the wrong thing, you get

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:02.740
sent to the concentration camp.

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:08.320
We're living in societies with a relative
degree of freedom.

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:12.370
Often infringed by authoritarian structures.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:18.529
But they don't have the kind of force they
do if the SS troops are standing behind you.

00:30:18.529 --> 00:30:21.130
We shouldn't compare ourselves with that.

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:27.289
In that case, what is an anarcho-syndicalist
take on education?

00:30:27.289 --> 00:30:30.590
On what it should look like and what needs
to change?

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:34.450
From how it is now to how it should be.

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:40.480
Actually, the anarchist movement, not just
the anarcho-syndicalists, were in the forefront

00:30:40.480 --> 00:30:41.919
of developing.

00:30:41.919 --> 00:30:43.240
Progressive education systems.

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:51.779
So for example, in Spain, where the 1936 revolution
was the most successful.

00:30:51.779 --> 00:30:54.560
So for example, of the anarchist revolution.

00:30:54.560 --> 00:31:02.480
That was preceded by decades of educational
efforts in villages and towns everywhere.

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:06.920
Trying to create a free, liberatory environment.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:10.510
In which students could find their own ways.

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:12.309
Their creativity would be sponsored.

00:31:12.309 --> 00:31:14.490
They would work jointly with one another.

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:19.640
Very similar to progressive education tendencies
of John Dewey and others.

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:23.019
Which I happened to benefit from as a child.

00:31:23.019 --> 00:31:26.610
In fact, I'm familiar with and taught that
way as well.

00:31:26.610 --> 00:31:29.529
So yes, anarchist education has been very
progressive.

00:31:29.529 --> 00:31:35.850
There's also people like Paulo Freire who
argued that it should be an interactive process.

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:38.600
Where the teacher is learning from the students.

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:40.169
Not just students.

00:31:40.169 --> 00:31:42.980
Having an opportunity for freedom.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:49.179
That's what I think is the kind of direction
that education should take.

00:31:49.179 --> 00:31:55.130
The course that it should follow and does
in the better places.

00:31:55.130 --> 00:32:04.470
Do you feel that if education is too centralized,
these alternative platforms will be sidelined?

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:07.620
Neglected in the public sphere?

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:16.460
Or for example, in universities that student
participation would get minimized?

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:18.899
I don't think there's a simple answer.

00:32:18.899 --> 00:32:21.720
You could have a public education system.

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:29.789
Which fosters individual creativity, freedom,
student initiative and so on.

00:32:29.789 --> 00:32:33.419
You could have a scattered system of charter
schools.

00:32:33.419 --> 00:32:38.769
Which are business run and which impose discipline.

00:32:38.769 --> 00:32:40.530
It depends on the educational program.

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.480
Not whether it's central or not.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:43.480
It depends.

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:50.320
I have a question that either you would think
is completely polar opposite or that they

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:51.580
completely overlap.

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:55.419
What were you thinking during your interview,
your conversation with Foucault?

00:32:55.419 --> 00:33:00.059
And what were you thinking during your conversation
with Ali G?

00:33:00.059 --> 00:33:01.059
With Ali G?

00:33:01.059 --> 00:33:02.059
I didn't hear the first one.

00:33:02.059 --> 00:33:03.970
With Foucault and with Ali G.

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:06.630
Well, it's quite different.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:12.260
In the case of Ali G, I was not particularly
interested in the interview.

00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:15.370
But I think it was the BBC or whoever was
behind it.

00:33:15.370 --> 00:33:18.809
They gave me a big song and dance about it.

00:33:18.809 --> 00:33:21.840
It was going to be a very serious interview.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:22.840
So I finally agreed.

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:26.340
As soon as he walked in, I realized this is
a joke.

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:28.470
I tried to be polite.

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:29.919
I had a hard time with it.

00:33:29.919 --> 00:33:31.269
I didn't take it seriously enough.

00:33:31.269 --> 00:33:34.710
You mean to say you realized that it was a
joke?

00:33:34.710 --> 00:33:39.210
Or you felt like the whole interview was a
joke and you thought he was for real?

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:45.139
The whole thing was obviously some kind of
an efforted comedy, which I didn't want to

00:33:45.139 --> 00:33:46.139
be part of.

00:33:46.139 --> 00:33:50.980
And I was perfectly, I mean, I was on the
tip of my tongue saying, look, this is enough.

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:51.980
Let's terminate it.

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:54.889
But being polite, I went along with it for
a while.

00:33:54.889 --> 00:33:56.330
Foucault is quite different.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:34:01.309
Actually, we had spent, Foucault and I had
spent a large part of the day together.

00:34:01.309 --> 00:34:03.500
The interview was in the evening.

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:07.260
I was just walking around the Dutch countryside.

00:34:07.260 --> 00:34:12.899
Partly because we wanted to have a chance
to talk, but partly to see if we could get

00:34:12.899 --> 00:34:16.929
by with him talking French and me talking
English.

00:34:16.929 --> 00:34:19.889
Would we be able to understand each other?

00:34:19.889 --> 00:34:20.889
Or should we have a translator?

00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:23.750
We finally figured we could carry it off.

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:25.609
I don't know much French.

00:34:25.609 --> 00:34:27.530
He doesn't know much English, but it worked.

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:34.440
And the debate was about issues that are fairly
serious issues.

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:35.890
So nothing before archaeology.

00:34:35.890 --> 00:34:37.340
We disagreed about a lot of things.

00:34:37.340 --> 00:34:40.950
I was kind of appalled by some of his views.

00:34:40.950 --> 00:34:44.990
But it was within the domain of rational discourse.

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:45.990
